The `ToUpper` method in .NET's `System.Globalization` namespace does not require more memory when the `destination` span has the same length as the `source` span. This is because the method allocates a new span of the same length as the source span, which can be smaller than the destination span if the source span is longer.

However, there are some edge cases to consider:

- If the source span is null, the method will throw a NullReferenceException.
- If the culture is null, the method will throw a NullReferenceException.
- If the source span is shorter than the destination span, the method will allocate a new span of the same length as the source span, which can be larger than the destination span.

To avoid these edge cases, you should always check for null values and handle them accordingly.

```csharp
public static string ToUpper(this ReadOnlySpan<char> source, Span<char> destination, CultureInfo culture)
{
    if (source == null || destination == null)
    {
        throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(source), "Source and destination spans cannot be null");
    }

    if (source.Length > destination.Length)
    {
        destination = stackalloc char[source.Length];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < source.Length; i++)
    {
        destination[i] = char.ToUpper(source[i]);
    }

    return new string(destination);
}
```

